notes:
Maintainer risk: MEDIUM — three real spec-conformance bugs (.1 channel via wrong method, .4 silent zero-defaults on required caps, .8 messageExpirySeconds nullability mismatch) plus a P0 test-integrity violation (.3 serverRetryAfter self-oracle). None are showstoppers but all four will produce wrong runtime behaviour or false-negative test results, and .1 in particular will break against any spec-compliant server.
Confidence: ~15% chance of a missed bug of P0-P1 severity. Least confident flags: .8 (messageExpirySeconds nullability — relies on a single spec line read; could be misread); .9 (queryChanges client/server mismatch is real per README, but the operational impact depends on which queryChanges methods are actually unbacked in jmap-chat-server, which I didn't verify in source). Uncertainty driven by: did not run cargo test --workspace or cargo clippy during this read-only pass (workspace tests may already cover something I flagged as untested); did not exhaustively cross-check every method handler against draft-atwood-jmap-chat-00 (spot-checked Chat/set, Message/set, Message/query, Space/set addChannels — confirmed the .1 finding solid). Would raise confidence if: (a) verified .8 against the spec's Patch object §2.4 (does optional UnsignedInt mean nullable in JMAP idiom?); (b) ran the existing smoke test suite to ground truth which paths are tested; (c) compared each chat-client method by name against jmap-chat-server's exposed handlers to validate .9 scope.
API contract risk: MEDIUM — four workspace-policy violations on missing #[non_exhaustive] (.31-.34) plus a P2 leak of serde_json::Error in Patch::map_entry (.35) and a P2 chrono::DateTime exposure in format_receipt_timestamp_at (.36) are the most consequential. The remaining 16 findings (3 unsealed traits, doc-impl mismatch on ChatWsFrame::Unknown, dual pub mod paths, SetResponse default-T elision, systemic missing # Errors sections, panic-via-.expect sites, input-type precondition gaps, BlobLookupEntry String-vs-Id, SessionClient concurrency contract unspecified, parse_chat_sse_block edge cases, server_retry_after absent-vs-malformed conflation, Vec<String> for typed values, Other(String) wire-collision, Patch<T> attribute footgun, Patch<T> non_exhaustive decision) are real but lower-stakes contract gaps a third-party caller could work around with awareness. Workspace-template propagation will be needed for the systemic ones (Errors sections, dual paths, SetResponse elision) — those are not chat-client-specific.
API contract confidence: ~20% chance of missed P1-P2 issue. Least confident flags: .43 (Patch<T> attribute footgun — title was initially wrong, corrected; serde's exact absent-key behaviour for custom-Deserialize Patch<T> needs a doctest to confirm), .46 (SetResponse default-T elision — could be a deliberate workspace pattern across all *-client crates rather than an oversight), .47 (pub mod dual paths — same: may be canonical-template choice), .52 (Other(String) collision is real but practical impact is narrow; close to opinion). Uncertainty driven by: did not run cargo doc to surface broken intra-doc-links or missing-docs warnings; did not exhaustively walk every method file checking which # Errors variants are actually reachable (the 44-methods claim is approximate, derived from method count, not per-method enumeration); did not verify by running tests whether SessionClient is Send+Sync (assumed yes from auto-derive over reqwest::Client + Session, but not measured); did not cross-check sibling *-client crates to confirm which findings are chat-client-specific vs systemic (.40, .46, .47 are likely systemic; if confirmed, those should be lifted to workspace-wide beads under JMAP-x2gd or a new workspace canonical-template epic). Would raise confidence if: (a) cargo doc clean of warnings; (b) per-method # Errors enumeration; (c) Send+Sync auto-trait inspection via cargo expand or a compile-time assertion; (d) review-workspace-api pass to surface cross-crate divergence on the same findings.
Idiom rating: ACCEPTABLE — no soundness issues, no unsafe, no Box<dyn Error> leaks; the diff is recognisably modern Rust (let-else, format-capture, transparent newtypes, #[non_exhaustive] used widely) with localised non-idiomatic patches: deferred-init bindings instead of match-as-expression (.53), pre-let-chains nested if-let pyramids (.54), DRY violations in chat_push handling (.55/.56) and capability accessors (.63), serde error-info loss into String (.57), legacy 'ref' patterns (.64), and three wire-enums begging for a macro (.61). The patterns the crate gets right (typed Patch<T>, redacting Debug impls, Clearable-via-Option, builder-pattern Input types) more than counterbalance the patches the crate gets wrong. A Rust practitioner reading this would nod through ~90% of the diff and reach for clippy --fix on the rest.
Rust idiom confidence: ~20% chance of missed P2-P3 idiom issue. Least confident flags: .54 (let-chains adoption — did not run clippy --fix to enumerate every nested if-let site; sampled space.rs/chat.rs/message.rs, may have missed sites in misc.rs/contact.rs/quota.rs), .61 (wire-enum macro — whether the same pattern exists in jmap-types and sibling crates needs a workspace-wide grep; if so the finding belongs in a workspace-template bead not chat-client), .63 (capability accessor consolidation — same workspace-template question), .64 ('ref' patterns — sampled chat.rs:59 etc. but did not enumerate every site; clippy --fix would surface them all). Uncertainty driven by: did not run 'cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings -W clippy::pedantic' which would mechanically surface the entire clippy-priority subset of these findings; did not run 'cargo expand' to confirm the closure-type-annotation finding (.66) reflects an actual inference issue versus author preference; did not check sibling *-client crates to determine which findings are systemic vs chat-client-specific (.61, .63 are almost certainly systemic). Would raise confidence if: (a) clippy pedantic run produces issues consistent with .54, .64, .58, .65; (b) workspace grep for 'fn .*_capability.*Result<Option' finds the same shape in jmap-mail-client/jmap-calendars-client (would lift .63 to workspace-template); (c) review-workspace-api pass surfaces the wire-enum-macro question across crates.
